WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.800
19 smartphone artificial intelligence models now beat GPT-4, putting last year's best tutor in your pocket. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 195. GPT-4 is a Chat program from the company OpenAI that writes, answers questions, and helps with schoolwork. For a full year it lived only in the cloud, which means it ran on huge distant computers you reach through the internet. Ethan Mollick, who has tracked these programs for years, noted that GPT-4 held the top spot that whole

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:31.840
time before smaller versions caught up.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:36.680
The new ones are small enough to sit on a regular phone, and they still handle those same

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:38.120
writing and question tasks.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:41.960
A giant shared computer in a library is the old picture of this.

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:45.020
The machine you had to visit and wait your turn to use.

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:50.080
What you carry now is closer to a super smart notebook that works at your desk on the

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:52.360
bus or with the Wi-Fi switched off.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:58.660
You no longer need fast internet or a paid subscription just to get strong help with homework, storywriting,

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:04.120
or game ideas. A student who wants instant feedback on an essay, or a quick coding hint,

00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:09.420
can get it without waiting on a far away server. There is no extra data cost when the answers

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:13.940
are born on the phone itself. Your questions and writing, sometimes called prompts,

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:19.500
stay on the device instead of traveling to distant servers. That can feel more private for everyday

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:24.240
school or creative work. The way a locked diary differs from a letter you drop in the mail.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:29.420
The helper also keeps working in places with spotty internet even when the connection drops.

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:34.020
For anyone thinking about future jobs, the skills to use and improve these

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:38.520
on-phone models will matter sooner than most people expect. You can already test

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:42.820
several of them on Android or iOS through free or low-cost apps that let you

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:48.080
chat offline. Search your App Store for on-device AI or for local large

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:53.420
language model demos. A large language model or LLM is a program that predicts

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:58.260
words so well it can hold a conversation and write. Open one of the free chat versions

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:02.560
for a few minutes and notice how quickly the answers arrive compared with tools that

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:07.840
only live in the cloud. Engineers are teaching a robot hand to spin a pen the way a kid learns

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:13.340
to juggle. Writing a bike is the closest everyday version of the same idea. You wobble,

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:18.020
you tip, you adjust your balance the next time, and slowly the corrections get smaller.

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:23.060
The robot hand practices that loop thousands of times inside a computer game before it

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:24.440
ever touches a real object.

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:29.540
Engineers build a pretend version of the hand inside a simulator, which is a practice

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:31.460
field made of math instead of metal.

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:36.500
In that pretend world, the hand tries spinning a pen or turning a cube over and over.

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:41.840
Every time it drops the object, the program gives it a low score, like missing a jump in

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:42.420
a video game.

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:47.280
Every time it succeeds, the score goes up, so the good finger movements get practiced

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:47.620
more.

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:47.720
More.

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:52.820
After millions of these tiny experiments, the hand figures out which motions work best.

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:57.020
Those instructions are then copied onto the real metal and plastic hand.

00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:01.080
The way you might learn to dance in a game, and then step it out on a real floor.

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:06.140
The physical hand can copy the moves without ever having practiced in the real world first.

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:11.040
It still makes small corrections on its own, the same way you lean left or right while

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:12.280
the bike is already rolling.

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:17.160
That whole process is called reinforcement learning, which just means learning from

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:20.040
a score instead of from a teacher spelling out every step.

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:24.740
It is also why the hand can do delicate tricks instead of only opening and closing.

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:30.640
The hand has 20 independently moving joints, like 20 tiny hinges that each get their own

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:31.340
little decision.

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:36.160
The training lets it copy human motions captured by a camera or a special glove.

00:03:36.820 --> 00:03:41.880
The open source code for this setup is available, so anyone curious can look at how the pretend

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:46.620
practice connects to the real hardware. Once you see the scoreboard, the fancy name

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:52.300
sits a lot lighter. Meta released two fresh chat models called Mews Wunder and Mews Wunderful.

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:57.100
The Verge reports. They add something called active memory and active focus.

00:03:57.740 --> 00:04:01.480
Active memory is the model remembering what you said earlier in the same chat,

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:05.160
like a classmate who still has your first sentence 10 minutes later.

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:10.160
Active focus is the model staying on the topic even when the conversation gets long.

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:13.800
long, instead of wandering off the group project and into lunch plans.

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:19.200
The models are free to try on Meta's platforms right now, and they aim to match the strongest

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:21.820
current systems while running on Meta's own services.

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:27.040
That gives you another free option beside paid tools, especially for longer creative

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:29.900
work like writing stories or outlining school reports.

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:35.600
Open Meta's AI website in any browser, start a new chat, and ask it to help you plan

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:40.480
a weekend trip. Midway through, change the destination and watch whether it keeps your

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:46.200
original plans without you repeating everything. Ethan Mollick also shared a short video of his AI

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:52.440
agent noticing a sign-up bug on his newsletter and quietly testing it on its own. An AI agent

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:57.000
is a helper that can take a goal and work through steps without you holding its hand the whole time.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:01.800
This one figured out the mistake and even suggested a fix before he had to do anything,

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:05.820
like a lab partner who spots a leak and starts checking before you ask.

00:05:06.260 --> 00:05:10.760
That kind of background help is still new and it shows where personal AI helpers are heading.

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:14.360
You can try a similar free agent today at the Po website.

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:19.860
Create a free account, pick the clawed bot or the Gemini bot, and give it a simple task

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:22.640
like check if this link works and tell me what is wrong.

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:28.080
The agent will attempt the task step by step and report back, which is a clear look at

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:32.860
small chores getting handled without constant instructions. A Reddit thread is full of

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:37.560
listeners who keep finding songs they enjoy, only to learn later that the music was made

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:42.680
by AI. The comments mix curiosity with worry about what that means for human artists.

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:48.360
Many people describe the same pattern. They add a track to a playlist, then feel conflicted

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:53.560
once they realize no person performed it. That's it for today. Remember, every AI expert

00:05:53.560 --> 00:05:58.360
started exactly where you are right now. If something we talked about today made you curious,

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:04.120
go try it. That's literally how learning works. Stay curious, keep experimenting, and we'll

